 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Why did John Moore-Brabazon tie a pig to his plane's wing?",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 2.914
  },
  {
   "text": "A wicker basket hangs off the wing, with a piglet inside wearing a sign.",
   "s": 3.214,
   "e": 6.896
  },
  {
   "text": "The sign says: I am the first pig to fly.",
   "s": 7.096,
   "e": 9.107
  },
  {
   "text": "If the engine coughs, the pig, the plane and the pilot all hit the mud.",
   "s": 9.308,
   "e": 12.466
  },
  {
   "text": "Everyone laughs. Airplanes are six years old and barely leave the ground.",
   "s": 12.666,
   "e": 16.206
  },
  {
   "text": "One man bets his top hat the pig never leaves the ground.",
   "s": 16.406,
   "e": 19.275
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it gets worse: the wind picks up, and the piglet starts squealing.",
   "s": 19.475,
   "e": 22.547
  },
  {
   "text": "The plane wobbles. The basket swings. The crowd covers their eyes.",
   "s": 22.747,
   "e": 26.129
  },
  {
   "text": "So why risk it? Because people kept saying one thing about flying machines.",
   "s": 26.529,
   "e": 30.266
  },
  {
   "text": "Sure, and pigs might fly. Meaning: never going to happen.",
   "s": 30.466,
   "e": 33.24
  },
  {
   "text": "So he takes off, circles the field, and lands the pig safe in the grass.",
   "s": 33.44,
   "e": 36.607
  },
  {
   "text": "The piglet trots out, the crowd cheers, and the old joke dies forever.",
   "s": 36.807,
   "e": 40.272
  },
  {
   "text": "So yes, pigs can fly. Ask John Moore-Brabazon.",
   "s": 40.472,
   "e": 43.281
  }
 ],
